**Appendix D: Software, Data, and Online Resources**

Sep 29, 2026 · Updated

This appendix provides a comprehensive guide to the computational and data resources essential for applied macroeconometrics. Section D.1 organizes software packages and toolboxes by econometric methodology, with cross-references to the relevant chapters where each method is developed. Section D.2 covers the principal data sources, organized by geographic coverage and data type. Section D.3 offers practical recommendations for software selection, working with survey data, and managing computational challenges. Section D.4 collects code repositories, author websites, and community resources. All resources mentioned are freely available unless otherwise noted and were actively maintained as of early 2026.
Navigating This Appendix
•	Software for a specific method → Section D.1, organized by methodology (VAR, DSGE, local projections, etc.).
•	Data for empirical work → Section D.2, covering US sources (including FRED), international databases, real-time vintages, financial markets, and surveys.
•	Choosing the right tool → Section D.3, with recommendations by use case.
•	Quick reference → Tables D.3–D.6 at the end of this appendix.
D.1 Software Resources by Methodology
This section organizes software packages by econometric methodology rather than by programming language. Each subsection includes cross-references to the relevant chapters where these methods are developed.
D.1.1 ARIMA and GARCH Models
Related chapter: Chapter 1 (Univariate Models)
ARIMA Models
R: forecast and fable. The forecast package (Hyndman and Khandakar, 2008) is the most widely used R package for univariate time series forecasting. It implements the Hyndman–Khandakar algorithm for automatic ARIMA order selection via stepwise AIC minimization, as well as exponential smoothing state space models (ETS), ARFIMA, TBATS, and neural network autoregressions. The companion textbook Forecasting: Principles and Practice (Hyndman and Athanasopoulos, 2021) provides thorough coverage of these methods and is freely available online at https://otexts.com/fpp3/. Its tidyverse-compatible successor, the fable package (O'Hara-Wild et al., 2024), implements the same models within the tsibble framework, making it particularly suited for handling and forecasting many related series simultaneously. Both packages include comprehensive model diagnostics and forecast accuracy evaluation.
•	CRAN (forecast): https://cran.r-project.org/package=forecast
•	CRAN (fable): https://cran.r-project.org/package=fable
Python. In Python, statsmodels provides the SARIMAX class for seasonal ARIMA with exogenous regressors, with full maximum likelihood estimation and diagnostics. For automatic model selection, the pmdarima package replicates the auto.arima functionality of R's forecast package. In Stata, ARIMA and SARIMA models are available through the built-in arima command, with postestimation tools for residual diagnostics and dynamic forecasting.
Stata. Stata supports ARCH and GARCH estimation via the built-in arch command (available since Stata 9), covering GARCH, EGARCH, TARCH/GJR-GARCH, NARCH, and SAARCH specifications, with a range of distributional options and postestimation tests for ARCH effects.
GARCH and Volatility Models
As discussed in Chapter 1, GARCH models (Bollerslev, 1986) and their extensions (EGARCH, GJR-GARCH, TGARCH, FIGARCH, and Realized GARCH) are the standard tools for modeling conditional heteroskedasticity in financial time series. They serve both as standalone volatility forecasting models and as components within ARMA-GARCH specifications for joint mean-variance modeling.
R: rugarch. The rugarch package (Ghalanos, 2024) is the most comprehensive R package for univariate GARCH modeling. It implements the full family of GARCH specifications—standard GARCH, EGARCH (Nelson, 1991), GJR-GARCH (Glosten et al., 1993), APARCH, FIGARCH, component GARCH, and Realized GARCH—and supports a wide range of innovation distributions, including normal, Student-t, skew-Student, generalized error distribution, and their skewed variants. The mean equation allows for ARFIMA dynamics and ARCH-in-mean terms. The package provides maximum likelihood estimation via C++ code, rolling backtesting, bootstrap forecasting, and Value-at-Risk evaluation tests. The companion rmgarch package extends estimation to multivariate specifications, including DCC-GARCH (Engle, 2002), GO-GARCH, and Copula-GARCH models.
•	CRAN (rugarch): https://cran.r-project.org/package=rugarch
•	CRAN (rmgarch): https://cran.r-project.org/package=rmgarch
R: fGarch and tseries. For simpler applications, fGarch (part of the Rmetrics suite) provides GARCH(p,q) and EGARCH estimation with a straightforward interface, while the tseries package includes garch() for basic GARCH fitting and is commonly used in introductory contexts alongside unit root tests (ADF, KPSS, PP).
Python. The arch package (Sheppard, 2024) provides GARCH model estimation in Python, implementing GARCH, EGARCH, FIGARCH, and Realized GARCH specifications with multiple distributional assumptions. It integrates naturally with NumPy and pandas objects and supports rolling estimation and Value-at-Risk backtesting. For ARIMA modeling combined with GARCH errors, the statsmodels SARIMAX can be combined with arch in a two-step workflow.
•	Documentation: https://arch.readthedocs.io
D.1.2 VAR and Structural VAR Analysis
Related chapters: Chapter 2 (VAR Models), Chapter 4 (Shock Identification), Chapter 5 (Bayesian VARs)
Three comprehensive MATLAB toolboxes have become standard references for VAR analysis in macroeconomics. Table D.1 summarizes their key features.
MATLAB Toolboxes
VAR Toolbox. Developed by Ambrogio Cesa-Bianchi from the Bank of England, this toolbox provides well-documented implementations of frequentist VAR methods with multiple identification schemes. It estimates reduced-form VARs via OLS and supports several structural identification strategies: Cholesky ordering, Blanchard–Quah long-run restrictions (Blanchard and Quah, 1989), sign restrictions following Rubio-Ramírez et al. (2010), external instruments/Proxy SVAR (Stock and Watson, 2012a; Mertens and Ravn, 2013), and combined sign restrictions with instruments. Inference is conducted through bootstrap confidence intervals (with multiple methods available), and the toolbox produces impulse responses, forecast error variance decompositions, and historical decompositions. It includes a helpful "VAR Primer" with slides and replication codes for Gertler and Karadi (2015), Uhlig (2005), and Stock and Watson (2001).
•	Available at: https://github.com/ambropo/VAR-Toolbox
