Vector-Autoregression. This MATLAB package, maintained by Lorenzo Menna, provides a comprehensive toolkit for estimating VAR models, covering structural and Bayesian VAR, GMM estimation, max share identification, sign restrictions, and counterfactual historical decomposition with channel restrictions.
•	Available at: https://github.com/lmenna84/Vector-Autoregression/
BVAR_ Toolbox. Developed by Fabio Canova and Filippo Ferroni, this is one of the most comprehensive Bayesian VAR packages, documented in "A Hitchhiker's Guide to Empirical Macro Models" (Canova and Ferroni, 2021). It is a recommended starting point for researchers new to Bayesian methods. The toolbox supports Minnesota, conjugate, and hierarchical priors with optimal hyperparameter selection, and covers a wide range of model classes, including standard BVARs, mixed-frequency VARs, factor-augmented VARs (FAVAR), and panel VARs with cross-sectional heterogeneity. Identification options include Cholesky, sign restrictions, narrative restrictions, and external instruments. Beyond VAR analysis, the toolbox also implements local projections, both classical and Bayesian, spillover and connectedness measures, trend-cycle decomposition, and business cycle dating. Forecasting capabilities include both point and density forecasts with comprehensive evaluation metrics. Extensive video tutorials are available on YouTube.
•	Available at: https://github.com/naffe15/BVAR_
BEAR Toolbox (European Central Bank). User-friendly Bayesian VAR toolkit developed by Alistair Dieppe, Romain Legrand, and Björn van Roye. Particularly suited for policy analysis and users who prefer a graphical interface. BEAR offers a graphical user interface that makes it accessible to nonprogrammers. On the estimation side, it supports Minnesota, normal-Wishart, Sims–Zha, dummy observation, and pandemic priors, as well as time-varying parameters and stochastic volatility (see Chapter 10). The toolbox also handles mixed-frequency estimation, factor-augmented VARs, and Bayesian Panel VARs with hierarchical priors. For policy work, it provides conditional forecasting for scenario analysis, equilibrium VARs for trend-cycle decomposition, and sign and magnitude restrictions for structural identification.
•	Documentation: Dieppe et al. (2016)
•	Available at: https://github.com/european-central-bank/BEAR-toolbox
•	ECB page: https://www.ecb.europa.eu/pub/research/working-papers/html/bear-toolbox.en.html
•	Pandemic priors: https://github.com/dcascaldi/pandemic_priors
BVAR Toolkit (Joshua Chan). Joshua Chan maintains a MATLAB toolkit for Bayesian VARs that complements the toolboxes above, together with a set of step-by-step tutorials on Bayesian macroeconometric methods.
•	Available at: https://github.com/joshuaccchan/bvar-toolkit
•	Tutorials: https://joshuachan.org/tutorials/
Applied Bayesian Econometrics for Central Bankers. Written by Andrew Blake and Haroon Mumtaz, these comprehensive lecture notes and MATLAB codes cover Bayesian VAR and DSGE estimation and are ideal for self-study.
•	Available at: https://www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/awdrimya898ay223o5ako/draft2.pdf?rlkey=sx34s9iickjqx9f4gd2u6awiv&e=1&dl=0
R Packages
R offers a rapidly growing ecosystem for VAR analysis, with packages providing both frequentist and Bayesian approaches.
bsvars. State-of-the-art Bayesian structural VAR package implemented in C++ for computational speed. Recommended for research requiring heteroskedasticity-based identification. This package, developed by Tomasz Woźniak, supports both homoskedastic and heteroskedastic specifications, with stochastic volatility (centered and noncentered parameterizations) and Markov-switching heteroskedasticity. It also accommodates nonnormal structural shocks (Student-t and mixture distributions) and provides identification through exclusion restrictions, heteroskedasticity, and nonnormality, with built-in verification tools. Estimation uses three-level hierarchical shrinkage priors. Two companion packages extend its functionality: bsvarSIGNs for sign-restricted identification and bpvars for Bayesian panel VARs.
•	CRAN: https://cran.r-project.org/package=bsvars
•	Website: https://bsvars.org
•	Reference: Woźniak (2024)
Additional R Packages. The vars package (Pfaff, 2008) provides standard frequentist VAR estimation with Granger causality tests, impulse responses, and forecast error variance decompositions—it is the workhorse for quick exploratory analysis. For Bayesian work, BVAR implements the Giannone et al. (2015) approach to automatic hyperparameter selection, treating the Minnesota prior hyperparameters as unknown and estimating them from the data (as discussed in Chapter 5). The bvartools package (Mohr, 2020) offers flexible BVAR estimation with multiple priors, SVAR identification, and factor models. Additional specialized packages include VARsignR for sign-restricted VAR identification, mfbvar for mixed-frequency Bayesian VAR with stochastic volatility (see Chapter 11), and bvarsv for time-varying parameter VARs following Primiceri (2005) (see Chapter 10).
Python and Stata
Python. Python's coverage of structural VAR methods remains less developed than MATLAB or R. For standard reduced-form analysis, statsmodels provides VAR, VECM, state space models, and comprehensive time series tools, while PyMC enables Bayesian VAR estimation through probabilistic programming (though it requires custom model specification). For advanced identification methods, researchers often adapt replication code from published papers or translate existing MATLAB/R implementations.
Stata. Stata provides the built-in commands var and svar for reduced-form and structural VAR estimation, and bayes:var for Bayesian VAR estimation (available from Stata 15 onward).
D.1.3 Local Projections
Related chapter: Chapter 3 (Local Projections)
Local projections (Jordà, 2005) provide a flexible alternative to VARs for impulse response estimation. As discussed in Chapter 3, they offer robustness to model misspecification at the cost of efficiency.
R: lpirfs. The most comprehensive package for linear and nonlinear local projections, developed by Philipp Adämmer. It supports linear local projections with Newey–West standard errors, state-dependent responses following Auerbach and Gorodnichenko (2012), panel data local projections, and instrumental variable estimation (LP-IV), and includes replication of Ramey and Zubairy (2018).
•	CRAN: https://cran.r-project.org/package=lpirfs
•	Reference: Adämmer (2019)
Stata Commands. Stata offers the native lpirf command for local projections (from Stata 17 onward), the SSC package locproj for panel data local projections, and lpdid for local projections difference-in-differences (Jordà and Taylor, 2024).
MATLAB. Local projections are included in the BVAR_ toolbox (Canova–Ferroni), and additional code is available on Òscar Jordà's website: https://sites.google.com/site/oscarjorda/home/local-projections.
D.1.4 DSGE Models
Related chapter: Chapter 6 (DSGE Models)
Dynare: The Industry Standard
Dynare remains the dominant platform for DSGE solution and estimation, continuously developed at CEPREMAP. As noted in Chapter 6, its widespread adoption means extensive documentation, active user forums, and compatibility with published replication codes. The current version (6.x) supports first-, second-, and third-order perturbation (with pruning) as well as perfect foresight simulations. Estimation capabilities include maximum likelihood and Bayesian estimation (Fernández-Villaverde and Rubio-Ramírez, 2007; Schorfheide, 2000) via Metropolis–Hastings and other samplers. For policy analysis, it offers optimal policy computation under both discretion and commitment, including Ramsey optimal policy. Identification and sensitivity analysis tools provide useful diagnostics. Notable extensions include handling of occasionally binding constraints (OccBin toolkit), mixed-frequency estimation, and particle filtering for nonlinear models. Dynare runs on MATLAB, GNU Octave, and testing in Julia.
•	Website: https://www.dynare.org
•	Forum: https://forum.dynare.org (essential for troubleshooting)
DSGE Models with Stochastic Volatility and Student-t Shocks
As an additional computational resource for readers interested in extending the DSGE methods discussed in the book, the DSGE-SVt MATLAB Toolbox provides tools for the Bayesian analysis of high-dimensional DSGE models with Student-t shocks and stochastic volatility.
The MATLAB® programs in this package perform Bayesian analysis of high-dimensional DSGE models with Student-t shocks and stochastic volatility. The toolbox simulates the posterior distribution using the tailored randomized-block Metropolis–Hastings (TaRB-MH) algorithm of Chib and Ramamurthy (2010), calculates the marginal likelihood using the methods of Chib (1995) and Chib and Jeliazkov (2001), and includes a range of post-estimation tools.
For further details, see the accompanying paper by Chib, Shin and Tan (2020), "DSGE-SVt: An Econometric Toolkit for High-Dimensional DSGE Models with SV and t Errors."
•	Code and documentation: DSGE-SVt MATLAB Toolbox on GitHub
DSGE Model Repositories
DSGE_mod Collection. Johannes Pfeifer maintains the most comprehensive public repository with 60+ replicated models. This is an invaluable resource for learning Dynare and verifying your own implementations. The collection includes landmark models such as Smets and Wouters (2007) (the benchmark medium-scale DSGE), Christiano et al. (2005) on nominal rigidities and monetary policy, Gertler et al. (2007) on the financial accelerator, as well as Aguiar–Gopinath (2007) on emerging markets, Jermann–Quadrini (2012) on financial shocks, and the textbook models from Galí (2015) that are particularly useful for teaching.
•	Available at: https://github.com/JohannesPfeifer/DSGE_mod
Macro Model Database (MMB). Systematic collection of models in a unified framework, enabling cross-model comparison of policy rules and shock transmission.
•	Website: https://www.macromodelbase.com
QuantEcon. Open-source lectures and code for quantitative economics in Python and Julia, covering both DSGE and other dynamic models.
•	Website: https://quantecon.org
Python Packages for DSGE
A growing ecosystem of Python packages now supports DSGE model solution, simulation, and Bayesian estimation. While none yet matches Dynare's breadth, several offer distinctive advantages—particularly modern sampling algorithms, JAX-accelerated computation, and native integration with the broader Python scientific stack.
gEconpy. Developed by Jesse Grabowski and inspired by the R package gEcon, gEconpy allows users to specify DSGE models in the optimization problem space using .GCN files: one writes down utility functions, constraints, and calibration blocks, and the package derives first-order conditions automatically. This substantially reduces the risk of algebraic errors and facilitates rapid prototyping—adding a term to the utility function, for example, requires modifying only a few lines rather than rederiving the entire model by hand. Bayesian estimation is performed via PyMC, supporting both Metropolis–Hastings and the gradient-based No-U-Turn Sampler (NUTS) through nutpie, which exploits JAX compilation for significant speed gains over traditional random-walk algorithms. The package also supports maximum likelihood and MAP estimation. A useful feature is the ability to export solved models to Dynare .mod files, enabling cross-validation against the standard platform.
•	Available at: https://github.com/jessegrabowski/gEconpy
•	Documentation: https://geconpy.readthedocs.io
pydsge. This package, developed by Gregor Boehl, is specifically designed for estimating and simulating DSGE models with occasionally binding constraints, such as the effective lower bound on interest rates (Boehl and Strobel, 2024; Boehl, 2022). It uses a hybrid nonlinear filter combining elements of the particle filter and the Kalman filter, and comes equipped with the Differential-Independence Mixture Ensemble (DIME) MCMC sampler—a global sampler that simultaneously serves as a multistart optimizer and posterior simulator, making preliminary mode-finding unnecessary. Of particular practical interest is the accompanying model library (projectlib), which includes ready-to-use YAML specifications of the Smets and Wouters (2007) model (rank.yaml), its extension with hand-to-mouth agents (TANK, tank.yaml), and versions with BGG-type financial frictions (frank.yaml, ftank.yaml). Models are specified in a Dynare-like YAML syntax with the model equations provided in log-linearized form.
•	Available at: https://github.com/gboehl/pydsge
•	Documentation: https://pydsge.readthedocs.io
•	Model library: https://github.com/gboehl/projectlib
dsgepy. A lightweight Python library, developed by Gustavo Amarante, for specifying, calibrating, solving, simulating, and estimating linearized DSGE models. The specification interface mirrors Dynare's symbolic approach, and solution relies on the Sims (2002) methodology. Estimation uses Bayesian MCMC. While less feature-rich than the packages above, it provides a fully self-contained, pure-Python implementation that may be attractive for teaching purposes.
•	Available at: https://github.com/gusamarante/dsgepy
PyMacLab. The Python Macroeconomics Laboratory supports solving nonlinear DSGE models using first- and second-order perturbation methods, based on Paul Klein's Schur decomposition approach and the Gomme–Klein method for second-order accuracy. It employs a Dynare-like model file parser and includes example models up to the complexity of Christiano et al. (2005). Development has slowed in recent years, but the codebase remains a useful reference.
•	Available at: https://github.com/escheffel/pymaclab
Direct PyMC Approach. For advanced users, it is also possible to build a custom DSGE estimation pipeline by combining individual Python components: a rational expectations solver (e.g., gensys or Blanchard–Kahn implementations available on GitHub), the Kalman filter from statsmodels or a custom implementation, and Bayesian sampling through PyMC or emcee. This modular approach requires more upfront effort but offers full flexibility over the estimation procedure, including the use of Sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) methods, custom proposal distributions, and non-standard state-space formulations.
Julia Packages for DSGE
Julia offers computational advantages with speed approaching C++ (Aruoba and Fernández-Villaverde, 2015), making it attractive for computationally intensive applications such as particle filtering or estimation with many shocks.
Dynare.jl. Native Julia rewrite compatible with standard .mod files. Supports PATH solver and PARDISO for improved performance.
•	Available at: https://github.com/DynareJulia/Dynare.jl
MacroModelling.jl. Modern Julia-native DSGE toolkit with advanced features developed by Thore Kockerols. It supports first-, second-, and third-order perturbation with pruning, gradient-based Bayesian estimation via automatic differentiation, and NUTS and HMC samplers that typically converge faster than random-walk Metropolis. An extensive model library is included (Kockerols, 2023).
•	Documentation: http://www.thorekockerols.eu/MacroModelling.jl/stable/
SolveDSGE.jl. Developed by Richard Dennis, SolveDSGE.jl is a Julia package for solving DSGE models using global solution methods, including perturbation, projection, and sparse grids.
•	Available at: https://github.com/RJDennis/SolveDSGE.jl
Other DSGE Software
Several additional platforms serve specialized needs. The IRIS Toolbox (MATLAB) supports the Global Projection Model Network used by several central banks. The RISE Toolbox (MATLAB, developed by Junior Maih) is designed specifically for regime-switching DSGE models. In R, gEcon provides general equilibrium modeling with symbolic differentiation. For Python users, Dolo/Dolang offers symbolic model specification and global solution methods.
D.1.5 Time-Varying Parameter and Regime-Switching Models
Related chapter: Chapter 10 (Time-Varying Parameters and Structural Change)
Time-Varying Parameter VAR
As discussed in Chapter 10, the TVP-VAR with stochastic volatility (Primiceri, 2005) has become the workhorse for studying evolving macroeconomic relationships.
R Packages. The bvarsv package implements the Primiceri (2005) specification with the Kim–Shephard–Chib sampler for stochastic volatility, while shrinkTVP applies shrinkage priors for time-varying parameters, directly addressing the overparameterization problem.
MATLAB Code. Several researchers maintain high-quality MATLAB implementations. Joshua Chan provides extensive TVP-VAR, stochastic volatility, and hybrid model codes (https://joshuachan.org/code.html), together with a BVAR toolkit (https://github.com/joshuaccchan/bvar-toolkit) and tutorials (https://joshuachan.org/tutorials/). Jouchi Nakajima offers TVP-VAR-SV with detailed documentation (https://sites.google.com/site/jnakajimaweb/program). Dimitris Korobilis maintains Bayesian multivariate methods, including large TVP-VARs (https://sites.google.com/site/dimitriskorobilis/matlab), and Gary Koop provides various Bayesian implementations (https://sites.google.com/site/garykoop/).
Markov-Switching Models
In R, MSwM provides univariate Markov-switching regression via the EM algorithm, while MSBVAR, by (Brandt, 2016), implements Markov-switching Bayesian VARs. In Python, statsmodels provides MarkovRegression and MarkovAutoregression classes for univariate models. It can be found at https://github.com/YaweiZhao/MSBVAR.
Smooth Transition Models
The R package tsDyn (Stigler and Di Narzo, 2024) provides a comprehensive nonlinear time series toolkit, including STAR, LSTAR, ESTAR, threshold VAR (TVAR), and threshold VECM specifications. For structural analysis, sstvars (Virolainen, 2024) implements structural smooth transition VARs.
D.1.6 Global VAR and Panel VAR
Related chapter: Chapter 13 (Panel and Global VARs)
Global VAR
The GVAR framework (Pesaran et al., 2004; Dees et al., 2007) models international linkages through trade-weighted foreign variables, enabling analysis of cross-country shock transmission. In R, BGVAR (Boeck et al., 2022) provides Bayesian GVAR estimation with sign restrictions and is the recommended choice for structural analysis, while GVARX implements the frequentist GVAR following Pesaran et al. In MATLAB, the GVAR Toolbox, developed by L. Vanessa Smith and Alessandro Galesi, is available at https://sites.google.com/site/gvarmodelling.
Panel VAR
For panel VAR estimation, R offers panelvar (GMM estimation) and bpvars (Bayesian panel VAR, part of the bsvars ecosystem). In Stata, the pvar command follows the Abrigo & Love approach.
D.1.7 Factor Models and High-Dimensional Methods
Related chapter: Chapter 8 (Factor Models and High-Dimensional Methods)
Dynamic factor models provide dimension reduction for large panels (Stock and Watson, 2002a,b). As discussed in Chapter 8, they underpin both FAVAR analysis and modern nowcasting.
R Packages. Key R packages for factor models and high-dimensional time series include the following. dfms (Krantz and Bagdziunas, 2023) provides efficient estimation of dynamic factor models via the EM algorithm following Doz et al. (2012) and Bańbura and Modugno (2014), and two-step estimation following Doz et al. (2011), with support for missing data and mixed-frequency settings. sparseDFM (Mosley et al., 2023) extends the standard DFM framework by constraining the loading matrices to be sparse, improving interpretability; it implements PCA, two-step, and EM estimation methods, including the novel sparse EM approach of Mosley et al. (2024). For real-time nowcasting, nowcasting (de Valk et al., 2019) implements dynamic factor model methods following Giannone et al. (2008) and Bańbura et al. (2011), providing tools to construct real-time data vintages and forecast economic variables at mixed frequencies. The R Package for Forecasting Models with Real-Time Data is at https://github.com/nmecsys/nowcasting. BigVAR (Nicholson et al., 2017b) estimates VAR and VARX models with structured convex penalties in high dimensions, including LASSO, elastic net, and hierarchical lag penalties, with penalty parameter selection via rolling cross-validation. fnets (Owens et al., 2024) implements factor-adjusted network estimation and forecasting for high-dimensional time series under a factor-adjusted VAR model, combining factor modeling with graphical structure estimation.
MATLAB. FAVAR implementations are included in both the BVAR_ and BEAR toolboxes described in Section D.1.2.
Python. The statsmodels.tsa.statespace.dynamic_factor module provides a state space formulation of dynamic factor models.
D.1.8 Machine Learning and Text Analysis
Related chapter: Chapter 9 (Machine Learning Methods)
Machine Learning for Economics
R Packages. The tidymodels framework provides a unified interface to random forests, boosting, neural networks, and other methods. For specific algorithms, xgboost implements gradient boosting (often the top performer in forecasting competitions), ranger offers fast random forests, and glmnet provides LASSO and elastic net regularization.
Python. Python offers the most mature ML ecosystem. The scikit-learn library provides a comprehensive ML toolkit with a consistent API. Specialized packages include XGBoost for gradient boosting, LightGBM (Microsoft) for fast gradient boosting, and tensorflow/keras and PyTorch for deep learning (the latter with dynamic computation graphs).
Text Analysis
For central bank communication analysis, policy uncertainty measurement, and sentiment extraction:
R Packages. The recommended starting point is quanteda for quantitative text analysis in economics applications. For tidy-data workflows, tidytext provides text mining tools. Topic modeling is available through topicmodels (Latent Dirichlet Allocation) and stm (structural topic models, which allow covariates to affect topic prevalence).
Python. spaCy provides industrial-strength NLP with pretrained models, while nltk (the Natural Language Toolkit) offers comprehensive but slower text processing. For topic modeling and word embeddings, gensim is the standard choice. At the frontier, transformers (Hugging Face) provides access to pretrained language models (BERT, GPT) for state-of-the-art text analysis.
D.2 Data Sources
This section organizes data sources by geographic coverage and type. We begin with US data (the most commonly used in academic research), then cover international sources, real-time data, financial markets, and surveys.
D.2.1 US Macroeconomic Data
FRED: Federal Reserve Economic Data
The Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis maintains the most comprehensive free US economic database. FRED should be your first stop for US macroeconomic data.
Coverage. FRED hosts over 840,000 time series from more than 118 sources, spanning national accounts, labor markets, prices, monetary aggregates, international data and exchange rates, and regional and state-level statistics.
Access Methods. FRED data can be accessed through its web interface at https://fred.stlouisfed.org, a free API (registration required; see https://fred.stlouisfed.org/docs/api/), and an Excel add-in for direct download into spreadsheets. Programmatic access is available in R via the fredr package, in Python via pandas-datareader or fredapi, and in MATLAB via getFredData.
Essential Series for Macroeconometrics. Table D.2 lists the series most commonly used in VAR and DSGE applications.
