Practical Tips for FRED Data. When downloading data from FRED, check the seasonal adjustment status: series ending in "SA" are seasonally adjusted, while "NSA" are not—most macroeconometric work uses SA data. For real-time analysis, use ALFRED (see below) to obtain data as it was known at specific dates, and always document your download date since GDP and employment data undergo regular revisions. Finally, note that real variables are expressed in base-year dollars (currently 2017), which affects level comparisons across long samples.
FRED-MD and FRED-QD
Curated panels designed specifically for forecasting and factor models (McCracken and Ng, 2016). These datasets are used extensively in Chapter 8 and provide the data for the FAVAR application in Section 8.14.
FRED-MD (Monthly). FRED-MD contains approximately 130 monthly series from 1959:01 to the present, organized into categories covering output and income, labor markets, housing, consumption and orders, money and credit, interest rates and spreads, prices, and the stock market. Prespecified transformation codes for stationarity are provided, and extracted factors are included in the download.
FRED-QD (Quarterly). FRED-QD provides 248 quarterly series from 1959:Q1 to the present, with greater national accounts detail and sectoral coverage than the monthly panel. Transformation codes consistent with FRED-MD are provided.
Access. Both datasets are available from the St. Louis Fed website at https://research.stlouisfed.org/econ/mccracken/fred-databases/, via the fbi package in R, or as direct CSV download with transformation codes.
Transformation Codes. The transformation codes ensure stationarity for factor extraction:
Code	Transformation
1	Levels (no transformation)
2	First difference: Δx_t
3	Second difference: Δ²x_t
4	Log: log(x_t)
5	Log first difference (growth rate): Δ log(x_t)
6	Log second difference: Δ² log(x_t)
7	Change in percent change: Δ(x_t / x_{t−1} − 1)
Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA)
The BEA produces the National Income and Product Accounts (NIPA)—the official source for GDP and its components.
Key Products. BEA produces GDP and its components (with quarterly advance, second, and third estimates, plus annual data), personal income and outlays (monthly), fixed asset tables showing capital stock by industry, input-output accounts, regional accounts at the state and metro area level, and international transactions data covering the current account and capital flows.
Access. Interactive tables are available at https://apps.bea.gov/iTable/, with API access at https://apps.bea.gov/api/. Vintage and historical data are also available for real-time analysis.
GDP Release Schedule. Understanding the release schedule is essential for real-time analysis. The advance estimate appears one month after the quarter ends (based on incomplete data), followed by the second estimate at two months and the third estimate at three months. Annual revisions each July typically revise the previous three years, and comprehensive revisions every five years can change the entire history.
Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS)
The BLS is the primary source for US labor market and price statistics.
Key Products. The BLS publishes the monthly Employment Situation report (combining establishment survey payrolls and household survey unemployment data), the Current Population Survey (CPS) providing household-level labor force data, the Job Openings and Labor Turnover Survey (JOLTS) reporting monthly job openings, hires, and separations, various price indices (CPI, PPI, Employment Cost Index), and productivity statistics including output per hour and unit labor costs.
Access. Data are available through the BLS data finder at https://www.bls.gov/data/ and an API at https://www.bls.gov/developers/. Microdata are accessible via IPUMS at https://ipums.org.
D.2.2 Real-Time and Vintage Data
Real-time data captures information actually available to forecasters and policymakers at each point in time. This is essential for fair evaluation of forecasting methods and understanding historical policy decisions.
Philadelphia Fed Real-Time Data Center
The most comprehensive real-time macroeconomic database for the US.
Key Products. The center's flagship product is the Real-Time Data Set for Macroeconomists (RTDSM), which provides quarterly vintages since 1965 for GDP, its components, prices, and employment. Each vintage shows the complete history as known at that date, making it essential for understanding how data looked when policy decisions were made. The center also hosts the Survey of Professional Forecasters (SPF) (see Section D.2.5), Greenbook Data containing Federal Reserve staff forecasts (available with a 5-year lag), and the Livingston Survey, a biannual expectations survey running since 1946.
Data Structure. Real-time data is organized as a matrix whose rows correspond to observation dates (e.g., 2020:Q1, 2020:Q2, …) and whose columns correspond to vintage dates (e.g., Nov 2020, Feb 2021, …). Each column shows the full history as known at that vintage.
Access. The data are available from the Philadelphia Fed at https://www.philadelphiafed.org/surveys-and-data/real-time-data-research, via the alfred package in R, and through Excel or text download organized by variable and vintage.
ALFRED: Archival FRED
ALFRED maintains historical vintages of all FRED series, allowing you to access any series as it appeared on any past date.
Features. ALFRED allows access to any FRED series as it appeared on any historical date, tracking revisions, benchmark changes, and seasonal adjustment updates. API access is available via the FRED API with a vintage date parameter.
Access. The web interface is at https://alfred.stlouisfed.org, and the alfred package provides access from R.
FRED-SD: State-Level Real-Time Database
Bokun et al. (2023) extend the ALFRED framework to US state-level data with FRED-SD, providing real-time vintages for state economic indicators.
Coverage. FRED-SD covers all 50 US states plus the District of Columbia with monthly and quarterly data on employment, income, housing, production, and other regional indicators. The database is particularly valuable for regional nowcasting, studying geographic heterogeneity in business cycles, and exploiting disaggregated information to improve national forecasts. Like ALFRED, FRED-SD preserves the complete revision history for all series.
Applications. State-level real-time data enables several important applications: (1) regional nowcasting using mixed-frequency models that combine monthly state employment with quarterly state GDP; (2) analysis of how national shocks propagate across states with different economic structures; (3) evaluation of state-level policy interventions accounting for data revisions; and (4) extraction of common factors from cross-state information to improve national forecasts.
Access. FRED-SD is available at https://research.stlouisfed.org/resources/fred-sd/ with API access through the standard FRED API by specifying state-level series codes.
OECD Real-Time Database
Coverage. The OECD real-time database covers OECD countries with vintages from the mid-1990s, including GDP, industrial production, unemployment, and prices. Data are accessible at https://stats.oecd.org under Main Economic Indicators → Original Release and Revisions.
D.2.3 International Data
IMF Data
World Economic Outlook (WEO). The WEO provides biannual forecasts (April and October) for 190+ countries covering GDP, inflation, fiscal balance, and the current account. Historical vintages are available for forecast evaluation. Access: https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/WEO.
International Financial Statistics (IFS). The IFS provides comprehensive financial and economic statistics—exchange rates, interest rates, prices, and national accounts—at monthly, quarterly, and annual frequency. Access: https://data.imf.org.
World Bank
World Development Indicators (WDI). The World Bank's WDI provides over 1,400 indicators for more than 200 countries, with long time series for some variables extending back to 1960. Coverage includes GDP, poverty, health, education, and infrastructure. Access: https://databank.worldbank.org.
OECD Statistics
Coverage. The OECD covers its 38 member countries plus key partners, with data on national accounts, labor markets, prices, and trade. The Main Economic Indicators (MEI) provide timely short-term data, and the STAN database offers industry-level statistics.
Access. Data are available through OECD.Stat at https://stats.oecd.org, the OECD package in R, and pandasdmx in Python.
Eurostat and ECB
Eurostat. Eurostat provides data for EU member states with harmonized definitions under ESA 2010. The database is available at https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/data/database, with access from R via the eurostat package.
ECB Statistical Data Warehouse. The ECB SDW covers euro area aggregates and individual country data, with particular strength in monetary statistics, interest rates, and bank lending. The website is https://sdw.ecb.europa.eu, accessible from R via the ecb package.
Bank for International Settlements (BIS)
Coverage. The BIS provides international banking statistics, credit to the nonfinancial sector (with long historical series), residential and commercial property prices, nominal and real effective exchange rates, and debt securities statistics. Access: https://www.bis.org/statistics/.
EA-MD-QD
EA-MD-QD (maintained by Matteo Barigozzi and Claudio Lissona) is the euro-area counterpart of FRED-MD and FRED-QD: a collection of large monthly and quarterly macroeconomic datasets for the euro-area aggregate and ten member countries (Austria, Belgium, France, Germany, Greece, Ireland, Italy, the Netherlands, Portugal, and Spain), designed for factor models and high-dimensional analysis. Each release provides seasonally adjusted series together with MATLAB code for frequency selection, missing-value imputation, stationarity transformations, and COVID-era outlier treatment. The dataset is updated monthly at https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21038097.
D.2.4 Financial Market Data
Interest Rates and Yields
FRED Series. Key interest rate series on FRED include daily Treasury yields at various maturities (DGS1, DGS2, DGS5, DGS10, DGS30), the federal funds rate (FEDFUNDS for the monthly average, DFF for daily), SOFR rates (replacing LIBOR) at various maturities, and Moody's corporate yields (AAA, BAA).
Gürkaynak–Sack–Wright Yield Curve. This dataset provides zero-coupon Treasury yields from 1961 to the present at daily frequency for maturities from 1 to 30 years. It is preferred to raw yields as it avoids coupon effects. The data are available from the Federal Reserve Board website.
Equity Markets
Free Sources. Yahoo Finance and Google Finance provide daily stock prices and indices, accessible programmatically via the quantmod package in R and the yfinance package in Python.
Key Indices via FRED. The S&P 500 is available as series SP500, the VIX (implied volatility) as VIXCLS, and the Wilshire 5000 as WILL5000IND.
High-Frequency Monetary Policy Data
These data are essential for the identification strategies discussed in Chapter 4.
Fed Funds Futures. Fed funds futures data are available from the CME Group (https://www.cmegroup.com), and historical data from Gürkaynak–Sack–Swanson are available on the authors' websites.
Preconstructed Monetary Policy Surprise Series. Several research teams have constructed and made available high-frequency surprise measures. Gürkaynak–Sack–Swanson provide target and path factors. Miranda-Agrippino–Ricco construct information-robust monetary shocks purified of Fed information effects. Nakamura–Steinsson offer policy news shocks. Jarociński–Karadi provide decomposed surprises separating monetary policy from information. Bu–Rogers–Wu construct shadow rate shocks for the zero lower bound period.
EA-MPD (ECB Monetary Policy Database). This database provides euro area high-frequency surprises around ECB announcements, decomposed into target rate, timing, forward guidance, and QE components. Access is through ECB working paper replication files.
Credit Spreads and Risk Measures
Corporate Spreads. The BAA–AAA spread on FRED is the classic credit risk measure. The Gilchrist–Zakrajšek (GZ) excess bond premium isolates risk appetite from default risk and is available from the Federal Reserve Board at https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/.
D.2.5 Survey-Based Data
Survey data provides direct measures of expectations, sentiment, and subjective uncertainty that cannot be inferred from market prices.
US Survey of Professional Forecasters (SPF)
The longest-running survey of macroeconomic forecasters in the US, essential for expectations research.
Coverage. The SPF has been conducted quarterly since 1968:Q4, with responses from 30–40 anonymous professional forecasters. It covers real GDP, inflation (multiple measures), unemployment, and interest rates at horizons ranging from the current quarter through four quarters ahead, with annual calendar-year and long-run (5–10 year) forecasts also available. Both individual-level and aggregate (median, mean) forecasts are published.
Unique Features. The SPF is distinguished by its density forecasts, in which respondents assign probabilities to outcome bins, enabling direct measurement of subjective uncertainty. Individual variance can be computed from these density forecasts, and both levels and growth rate point predictions are available.
Access. The data are hosted by the Philadelphia Fed at https://www.philadelphiafed.org/surveys-and-data/spf, accessible via the spfR package in R, and available as Excel downloads organized by variable.
University of Michigan Consumer Sentiment
This household survey measures consumer confidence and inflation expectations.
Coverage. Published monthly since 1952 (with both preliminary and final releases each month), the survey reports the Index of Consumer Sentiment (combining current conditions and expectations) and both 1-year and 5–10 year inflation expectations. Microdata are available for subscribers.
Access. The overall index is available on FRED as UMCSENT and 1-year inflation expectations as MICH. The survey website is http://www.sca.isr.umich.edu.
Conference Board Consumer Confidence
An alternative household sentiment measure with different methodology than Michigan.
Coverage. Published monthly since 1967, the survey reports a Present Situation Index and an Expectations Index, with questions covering labor market and business conditions.
Access. Available on FRED as CONCCONF (overall) and CONEXPEC (expectations component), and from the Conference Board at https://www.conference-board.org.
Blue Chip Economic Indicators
A commercial survey of business economists, widely used as benchmark for professional forecasts.
Coverage. Published monthly since 1976, it aggregates forecasts from 50+ forecasters at major financial institutions and corporations. Variables include GDP, inflation, interest rates, and unemployment, with near-term (current and next quarter) and annual calendar year forecasts.
Access. Subscription is required (https://www.wolterskluwer.com/en/solutions/blue-chip), though historical consensus data are available through the Philadelphia Fed.
ECB Survey of Professional Forecasters
The Euro Area counterpart to the US SPF, conducted quarterly since 1999. It covers HICP inflation, real GDP growth, and unemployment at horizons of 1 year, 2 years, and 5 years ahead, plus long-term expectations. Density forecasts are also available. Access: ECB Statistical Data Warehouse at https://sdw.ecb.europa.eu.
Other International Surveys
Bank of England. The BoE conducts the Inflation Attitudes Survey of household expectations (since 2001) and the Decision Maker Panel measuring business uncertainty (since 2016).
ZEW Economic Sentiment (Germany). A monthly survey of approximately 350 financial analysts covering the current situation and 6-month expectations. Access: https://www.zew.de; FRED: ZEWEZONE, ZEWGERMANY.
Ifo Business Climate (Germany). A monthly survey of approximately 9,000 firms covering the current situation and 6-month expectations across manufacturing, construction, trade, and services. Access: https://www.ifo.de.
D.2.6 Alternative and Big Data
Text-Based Indicators
Economic Policy Uncertainty (EPU). The EPU index is a news-based uncertainty measure constructed from newspaper coverage, available monthly from 1985 for the US and for 25+ other countries. Access: https://www.policyuncertainty.com. Related indices from the same source include the Monetary Policy Uncertainty Index, Trade Policy Uncertainty Index, Partisan Conflict Index, and Geopolitical Risk Index.
Alternative Data Sources
Google Trends. Google Trends provides search volume indices for terms and topics that can be useful for nowcasting (e.g., unemployment claims, retail sales). Access: https://trends.google.com and the gtrendsR package in R.
High-Frequency Economic Indicators. The COVID-19 pandemic spurred the development of timely alternative indicators, including Our World in Data (https://ourworldindata.org), Google Mobility Reports, and Opportunity Insights credit card spending data.
D.3 Practical Guidance
D.3.1 Software Recommendations by Use Case
For Standard Bayesian VAR Analysis. Users who want a graphical interface should start with the BEAR toolbox in MATLAB. Those who need speed and modern heteroskedasticity-based methods should use bsvars in R. For the most comprehensive feature set and documentation, the BVAR_ toolbox in MATLAB is recommended.
For Frequentist VAR. For quick exploratory analysis, the vars package in R is the simplest option. Researchers seeking replication codes for published papers will find the VAR Toolbox in MATLAB most useful. Stata users benefit from the well-documented built-in var/svar commands.
For DSGE Estimation. Dynare in MATLAB or Octave is the standard choice due to its wide adoption. For computational speed, Dynare.jl or MacroModelling.jl in Julia are preferred. For learning with worked examples, Pfeifer's DSGE_mod repository is invaluable. For DSGE models with Student-t shocks and stochastic volatility, the DSGE-SVt MATLAB toolbox (Section D.1.4) provides a dedicated Bayesian toolkit.
For Local Projections. In R, the lpirfs package provides the most comprehensive implementation. In Stata, the native lpirf command (from Stata 17) is available. In MATLAB, local projections are included in the BVAR_ toolbox.
For Reproducible Research. The best workflow for documentation is R packages combined with RMarkdown or Quarto. Python users benefit from Jupyter notebooks. Version control through Git integration is recommended on any platform.
For Computationally Intensive Work. DSGE estimation with many shocks or nonlinear filtering is best done in Julia. Large-scale Bayesian estimation likewise benefits from Julia or optimized C++. For parallel computing, the MATLAB Parallel Computing Toolbox and Julia (with native parallelism) are the main options.
For Machine Learning Integration. Python offers the best ecosystem (scikit-learn, TensorFlow, PyTorch). In R, the tidymodels framework provides a unified interface, and HDeconometrics is designed specifically for economists.
D.3.2 Working with Survey Data
Constructing Standard Measures
Consensus Forecast. The median or mean across forecasters at time t for horizon h:
\bar{F}_t^h = \frac{1}{N_t} \sum_{i=1}^{N_t} F_{i,t}^h. \qquad \text{(D.1)}
The median is often preferred as it is robust to outliers.
Forecast Disagreement. The cross-sectional standard deviation measures disagreement:
D_t^h = \sqrt{\frac{1}{N_t - 1} \sum_{i=1}^{N_t} \left(F_{i,t}^h - \bar{F}_t^h\right)^2}. \qquad \text{(D.2)}
Disagreement is often interpreted as a proxy for uncertainty, though the relationship is imperfect.
Subjective Uncertainty. For surveys with density forecasts (SPF, Michigan), individual uncertainty can be computed:
U_{i,t}^h = \sqrt{\sum_k p_{i,t,k}^h \left(m_k - F_{i,t}^h\right)^2}, \qquad \text{(D.3)}
where pʰᵢ,ₜ,ₖ is the probability forecaster i assigns to bin k, and mₖ is the bin midpoint.
Forecast Revision. The change in forecast for a fixed target date:
R_t^h = F_t^h - F_{t-1}^{h+1}. \qquad \text{(D.4)}
Revisions reveal new information incorporated by forecasters.
Testing Forecast Properties
Rationality Tests. Under rational expectations, forecast errors should be unpredictable:
y_{t+h} - F_t^h = \alpha + \beta X_t + \varepsilon_{t+h}. \qquad \text{(D.5)}
Test H₀: α = 0, β = 0 jointly. Rejection indicates inefficient use of information in Xₜ.
Efficiency Tests. Forecasts should fully incorporate past information:
y_{t+h} - F_t^h = \gamma \left(F_t^h - F_{t-1}^{h+1}\right) + \varepsilon_{t+h}. \qquad \text{(D.6)}
Test H₀: γ = 0. Rejection suggests forecasters under- or over-react to new information.
Sticky Information Test. Following Mankiw and Reis (2002), test for infrequent forecast updating:
\left(F_{i,t}^h - F_{i,t-1}^{h+1}\right) = \lambda \left(F_t^{CB,h} - F_{i,t-1}^{h+1}\right) + \varepsilon_{i,t}, \qquad \text{(D.7)}
where Fₜᶜᴮ,ʰ is a benchmark (e.g., central bank forecast). λ < 1 indicates sticky updating.
Using Surveys in Structural Analysis
News vs. Noise Shocks. Survey data can help distinguish news shocks (where both forecasts and outcomes move together) from noise shocks (where forecasts move but outcomes do not).
Purifying Monetary Policy Instruments. As discussed in Chapter 4, Miranda-Agrippino and Ricco (2021) show how to orthogonalize high-frequency surprises with respect to Fed information to isolate pure monetary policy shocks.
Expectations as VAR Variables. Survey expectations are increasingly incorporated directly into structural models: consumer confidence in consumption VARs, inflation expectations in Phillips curve analysis, and forecast disagreement as an uncertainty proxy in uncertainty VARs.
D.3.3 Data Quality Considerations
Timing Issues. Survey collection dates matter for real-time analysis: information sets differ across surveys (for example, the SPF is collected mid-quarter, while Michigan runs continuously). Timing assumptions should always be documented in published research.
Definition Changes. GDP definitions change over time (for example, intellectual property was capitalized in the 2013 comprehensive revision), and survey questions may change wording. Researchers should check documentation carefully for structural breaks in series definitions.
Panel Attrition. Not all forecasters respond every period, and panel composition changes can affect both consensus and disagreement measures. Individual fixed effects help but do not fully resolve composition bias.
Herding and Strategic Behavior. Forecasters may herd toward the consensus due to career concerns, and extreme forecasts may be avoided because of reputational risk. Robust statistics, such as the median or trimmed mean, are generally preferred for constructing consensus measures.
D.3.4 Computational Considerations
Language Performance. Aruoba and Fernández-Villaverde (2015) compare programming languages for DSGE model solution and find that Julia performs within 2.5× of optimized C++ (making it excellent for most applications), while MATLAB is approximately 10× slower than C++, and Python and R are significantly slower without careful optimization. In practice, the choice of language depends on the task: for standard BVAR estimation, any platform works well since the bottleneck is usually MCMC rather than matrix operations; for particle filtering and large-scale Bayesian estimation, Julia or C++ are recommended; and for machine learning integration, Python offers the most mature ecosystem.
Memory Management. When working with large datasets, R users should prefer data.table over base data frames, Python users should use pandas with appropriate dtypes (and consider dask for out-of-memory data), and MATLAB users should preallocate arrays and avoid growing arrays in loops. For long MCMC chains, storing thinned chains (every k-th draw), writing to disk periodically to prevent memory overflow, and using binary formats (HDF5, .rds, .mat) for large outputs are all advisable.
Cloud and High-Performance Computing. Some tasks in macroeconometrics—particularly particle filtering for nonlinear DSGE models, large-scale TVP-VAR estimation with stochastic volatility, and machine learning methods applied to high-dimensional panels—can exceed the memory or processing capacity of a standard laptop. For such applications, cloud computing platforms offer a practical solution. Google Colab (https://colab.research.google.com) provides free access to GPU-accelerated Python notebooks, which is particularly useful for deep learning and computationally intensive forecasting exercises. Amazon SageMaker and Microsoft Azure ML offer scalable environments for larger projects, though these require paid accounts. Many universities also maintain high-performance computing (HPC) clusters that provide access to multicore processors and large memory allocations at no cost to researchers; students should check with their institution's research computing services. When moving to cloud or HPC environments, containerization tools, such as Docker, help ensure that code, dependencies, and package versions are fully portable across machines, which also benefits reproducibility.
D.4 Online Resources and Code Repositories
D.4.1 Author Websites with Code
Several researchers maintain extensive, well-documented code repositories that serve as essential complements to their published work. Gary Koop provides code for Bayesian econometrics, including TVP, stochastic volatility, and model averaging (https://sites.google.com/site/garykoop/home/computer-code-2). Dimitris Korobilis maintains code for TVP-VAR, factor models, and machine learning for macro (https://sites.google.com/site/dimitriskorobilis). Joshua Chan offers state space models, stochastic volatility, and efficient samplers (https://joshuachan.org/code.html), a MATLAB BVAR toolkit (https://github.com/joshuaccchan/bvar-toolkit), and a series of tutorials on Bayesian macroeconometrics (https://joshuachan.org/tutorials/). Silvia Miranda-Agrippino provides BVAR, Bayesian LP, and Proxy SVAR code (https://silviamirandaagrippino.com/code-data). Haroon Mumtaz shares Bayesian VAR, TVP-VAR, and factor model implementations (https://sites.google.com/site/hmumtaz77/code). Katerina Petrova provides TVP-VAR and TVP-DSGE code (https://sites.google.com/site/katerinapetrovawebpage/research). James LeSage offers a comprehensive Econometrics Toolbox for MATLAB that covers spatial econometric estimation, Bayesian methods, time-series and VAR modeling, and regression analysis (https://www.spatial-econometrics.com). Finally, Òscar Jordà maintains code for local projections and smooth LP (https://sites.google.com/site/oscarjorda).
D.4.2 Community Resources
Useful community-maintained resources include QuantEcon (https://quantecon.org), which offers open-source Python and Julia lectures on quantitative economics; r-econometrics.com, with R tutorials for BVAR, SVAR, and factor models; the Forum Applied Macro (https://forumappliedmacro.wixsite.com/website/forum), a community-curated collection maintained by Mariarosaria Comunale; Awesome-VAR (https://github.com/kvasilopoulos/awesome-var), a curated GitHub list of VAR resources maintained by Kostas Vasilopoulos; and EconMacro (https://www.jamelsaadaoui.com/tag/stata-code/), a blog with codes, mainly in STATA and discussion about macroeconometric models applied to international economics, maintained by Jamel Saadaoui.
D.4.3 Replication Archives
Major journals now require the availability of code and data. The most important archives are the AEA Data and Code Repository for AER and AEJ journals (https://www.openicpsr.org/openicpsr/aea), the Journal of Applied Econometrics Data Archive (http://qed.econ.queensu.ca/jae/), the general-purpose openICPSR repository from the Inter-university Consortium for Political and Social Research (https://www.openicpsr.org), and Harvard Dataverse (https://dataverse.harvard.edu).
Quarto
Quarto (https://quarto.org), the next-generation successor to RMarkdown developed by Posit, is rapidly becoming the standard for reproducible scientific documents. Unlike RMarkdown, Quarto is language-agnostic: a single .qmd file can combine R, Python, and Julia code chunks with narrative text, LaTeX equations, and citations, and render to HTML, PDF, or Word from one source. Existing .Rmd files can be rendered without modification. Quarto integrates with RStudio, VS Code, Positron, and Jupyter Lab, and its visual editor makes it accessible to students who are not comfortable writing raw markdown. For textbook companion codes hosted on GitHub, Quarto documents provide an ideal format: students clone the repository, run quarto render, and obtain a fully reproducible document with all results regenerated from source.
